Evidence for ToyOSOrg/ToyOS#933: the T14's first download after boot.

## The instrument boot (`instrument/`)

Image: wt/toyos-first-run-instrument at 817338be3adb764022f4364dd5839632b7e3518d
(origin/main ca0b2472a + the internet_download row + probes; never lands),
image SHA-256 c34147ca6a2fc79aa0234f6fe55ec259cc6835a4bb58d288d069191f41a4c375,
staged with `cargo test --test toyos-build -- --metal --metal-readback <dir>
internet_download` (`staging.log`, exit 2 = staged), booted on the T14 by the
orchestrator (`manifest.txt`, `runner.log`: boot rc=0, judge EXIT=0).
Masked: LAN/DNS addresses, MAC, stick serial, GUIDs, root id, machine
product and BIOS; Fastly nodes appear only as 3-byte hashes.

`download/kernel.log` carries:
- `https_download: first-run-head` / `first-run run=<n>`: Fastly's x-cache,
  x-cache-hits, age, node hash, time to headers, the boot-clock stamps of the
  request and the first body byte, the body's MB per 250 ms, read waits.
- `netstack: probe t_ms=<boot ms>` every 250 ms while traffic moved: pass,
  message and frame counts, frames found by passes no message began, MAC
  counts (wire_seen = every frame the MAC saw, missed), and TCP's receive
  side (segments, out-of-order, old retransmitted text, ACKs, SACKs, window
  offered and its least, capacity, most unread, RTT estimate).

## Reading

Every run's headers: `x_cache=HIT x_cache_hits=1`, a different node each run,
head_ms 63-67, tcp 14-18 ms, tls 33-35 ms.

Run 1 (request at boot 15562 ms, body at 15565 ms): 2.803 s, 486 Mb/s; runs 2-6
1.573-1.688 s, 808-867 Mb/s. Its MB per 250 ms:
`[9.8 21.8 29.3 29.4 29.0 10.6 0.0 11.9 18.1 0.0 4.2 6.3]` against run 2's
`[12.9 29.6 29.4 29.4 29.4 29.4 10.5]`: line rate (29.4 MB per 250 ms) for
1.25 s, then two pauses; max read wait 561 ms (runs 2-6: 20-22 ms).

The probe lines across the pauses (cumulative counts):

| t_ms | wire_seen | segs | ooo | old | acks | sacks | wnd | min_wnd | max_unread |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 17056 | 92931 | 92866 | 1926 | 30 | 8379 | 947 | 4144384 | 4132992 | 75472 |
| 17463 | 92940 | 92872 | 1926 | 30 | 8380 | 947 | 4185856 | 4185856 | 8407 |
| 17713 | 113603 | 113536 | 1926 | 30 | 10072 | 947 | 4177152 | 4128768 | 79744 |
| 17963 | 114039 | 113974 | 1926 | 30 | 10109 | 947 | 4187136 | 4175744 | 35600 |
| 18279 | 114049 | 113982 | 1926 | 30 | 10110 | 947 | 4182912 | 4182912 | 11392 |

In the first pause (about 400 ms) the I219 saw 9 frames on the wire, all
taken (missed=0, overruns=0, starved=0); the window offered never fell below
4.13 MB; nothing waited unread; no new out-of-order segment, no retransmission
of text already held (old stays 30), no SACK. When the flow resumed it went
straight back to line rate in order. The receiver had told the sender it could
send 4 MB and had acknowledged everything that arrived: the sender stopped.
Interrupt delivery was regular throughout: messages track passes, and frames
found by passes no message began are 99 of 121586 over run 1.

The early loss episode (ooo 1926, 947 SACK-carrying ACKs in the first 450 ms)
is ordinary slow-start overshoot and recurs in run 2 (ooo +4021) without a
pause.

## The tcp change (head defd00bfbd67d37a57f05809e284adba5eaa1416)

- `host/host-defd00bfbd67d37a57f05809e284adba5eaa1416.log`: `cargo run -- --ci host`, EXIT=0, 86 steps green.
- `host/host-75e4a1114-red.log`: the first version (seed for both samplers),
  EXIT=1: `the_receive_window_grows_by_what_is_read_per_round_trip` offered
  1,071,640 of 8,000,000 bytes without timestamps, since the least fill time
  can never rise above a seed. Kept: it is why the seed is the timestamped
  estimate's only. (The loom failures in it are the suite's own controls.)
- `controls/control-unseeded.log`: at defd00bfbd67d37a57f05809e284adba5eaa1416, `cargo test --lib rx::` under
  `control-unseeded.patch` (the seed ignored): EXIT=101,
  `a_reply_the_peer_sat_on_does_not_set_the_round_trip` fails; restored.

The tcp change is not the cause of the first-run pause (the probe lines show
the estimate at 17 ms from the first line on); it is the unbounded first sample
the owner asked to be shown and fixed on its own.
